  "query_preview": "(This evening through Saturday)...\nIssued at 203 PM EDT Sat Oct 24 2026\n\nSurface analysis this afternoon shows strong high pressure centered \nover eastern PA, eastern NY and the upper St. Lawrence river valley. \nThis large high was extending its influence west across Indiana to \neastern TX and Eastern OK. A frontal boundary was found over the \neastern Dakotas and the high plains. High, thin CI was found across \nmuch of Indiana, providing filtered sunshine. Water Vapor showed \nstrong ridging still in place aloft over the upper Mississippi \nValley and western Great Lakes, but a tropical plume of moisture \nwas noted on the back side of the ridge, streaming north across the \nhigh plains. \n\nTonight...\n\nThe strong ridge axis aloft to the west is expected to slide \neastward overnight. This continue to protect Indiana from any \nforcing dynamics, steering them well to the north. This will result \nin the strong surface high to the east to the sink to the middle \nAtlantic states, but continue to dominate Indiana/s weather with dry \nlower levels and light southeast winds. Forecast soundings through \nthe night show a dry column, with some passing saturation aloft, \nwhich is indicative of high passing CI. Thus mostly clear skies will \nbe expected tonight, with persistence type lows in the middle to \nupper 40s.\n\nSunday through Monday...\n\nThe strong upper ridge that will be over Indiana tonight is \nexpected to stall and remain across the area on Sunday through \nMonday. This will once again steer forcing dynamics well north of \nIndiana and keep a dry air mass in place. The surface high will \nremain over the middle Atlantic states, providing southeasterly \nlower level flow on Sunday becoming southwest on Monday. \nFurthermore, by late Monday a new area of strong, Canadian high \npressure is expected to arrive across the Great Lakes on Monday \nNight as a cuspy area between high pressure systems passes. Forecast \nsoundings through Monday show dry lower and mid levels with \nsubsidence in play beneath the ridge. Only a few passing high CI \nwill be noted. Thus more mostly sunny days and mostly clear nights \nwith above normal temperatures.\n\nTuesday through Saturday...\n\nMore dry fall weather with above normal temperatures is expected \nduring the week ahead. \n\nModels show the upper ridge axis over Indiana on Tuesday shifting \nfarther east by Thursday. Meanwhile the upper flow containing any \nforcing dynamics remains well north of Indiana, across Canada. By \nThursday through Saturday, a deep upper low is expected to set up \nover the Pacific NW. This will result in a quick tropical flow aloft \nto stream across the American SW before reaching a ridge axis over \nthe Plains and Great Lakes. Little in the way of forcing looks to \narrive within this southerly jet aloft until Saturday, when a weak \nwave looks to pass across Indiana. Confidence in this 7 days out is \nlow.\n\nWithing the lower levels, strong high pressure will once again \ndevelop on the lee side of the upper ridge, providing dry weather \nfor the country for most points east of the Mississippi. Southerly \nlower level flow looks to return on Friday and Saturday, which may \nlead to the warmest days of the week. Furthermore, the southerly \nflow on Saturday in combination with the possible upper support may \nprovide additional support for rain. Thus overall, expect dry \nweather weather for Tuesday through Friday and some very low \nconfidence chances for rain on Saturday. Temperatures will remain \nabove seasonal normals.",
